WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.566
Endpoint security policies
aren't a higher-priority source;

2
00:00:03.566 --> 00:00:06.800
they have the same standing
as configuration profiles.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.619
In the program's library, though, they
own every Defender, BitLocker, firewall,

4
00:00:11.619 --> 00:00:13.990
account protection and LAPS setting,

5
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:18.350
and the settings catalog profiles
that duplicated them are retired.

6
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.412
The slide walks through each one.

7
00:00:20.412 --> 00:00:21.638
A few highlights.

8
00:00:21.638 --> 00:00:26.173
Sending every file sample to Microsoft
can upload organizational data,

9
00:00:26.173 --> 00:00:27.987
so that's the data owner's call.

10
00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:32.203
Silent encryption and a TPM PIN
can't apply to the same device,

11
00:00:32.203 --> 00:00:34.900
so Tier 0 gets its own BitLocker policy.

12
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.437
On an Entra-joined device, the
firewall's domain profile doesn't apply.

13
00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:43.557
Attack surface reduction rules
run in audit for thirty days,

14
00:00:43.557 --> 00:00:46.688
then warn for two weeks,
then block, ring by ring.

15
00:00:46.688 --> 00:00:50.950
And Windows LAPS now manages a
custom local administrator account,

16
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.613
with the built-in Administrator disabled.

17
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:56.017
Appendix A flags the crosswalk traps,

18
00:00:56.017 --> 00:01:00.020
such as a logon message breaking
Autopilot pre-provisioning.
